Most Angular developers write their entire career using exactly two directives, ngIf and ngFor, and never once build one of their own.
Press enter or click to view image in full size

I was that developer for three years. Then a senior engineer showed me a fifteen line directive that replaced two hundred lines of repeated DOM manipulation scattered across our app, and I felt genuinely embarrassed I had never bothered to learn this corner of the framework properly.
Directives are not an advanced topic. They are an ignored one. Nine tricks, each one something I now reach for constantly, and each one something almost nobody teaches you past the first tutorial.
Trick 1: HostListener and HostBinding Instead of Manual DOM Code
The problem
The instinct many developers reach for first, when they want an element to react to an event, is direct DOM manipulation inside a component.
ngAfterViewInit() {
this.el.nativeElement.addEventListener('mouseenter', () => {
this.el.nativeElement.style.backgroundColor = 'lightyellow';
});
}This works, but it bypasses Angular entirely, skips cleanup, and cannot be reused anywhere else without copying the whole block.
